\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quality estimation framework.}
    We systematically evaluate five confidence signals from three families---structural, surface, and model-internal---for LLM-based NER across four benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, two model families, and a 4B scale ablation.
    Structural signals reliably predict output quality (VC $\rho{=}0.38$ on the primary test bed; EM AUROC${=}0.82$ on CoNLL), with difficulty-dependent but model-stable rankings that support selective prediction and error flagging.

    \item \textbf{Diagnosis of the correlation-selection gap.}
    Quality estimation does not translate to structural selection: despite 12--14~pp oracle headroom, no structural signal significantly outperforms greedy decoding on any dataset ($p{>}0.05$), a gap confirmed by bootstrap testing at $N{=}32$.
    We trace this to two mechanisms---instance degeneracy (10--58\% of instances yield identical outputs, scaling with model capability) and LP compression (median within-instance range 0.041 nats)---and confirm via a diagnostic chain (LoRA-rank ablation, free-form decoding, entity-token LP, supervised signal combination) that the structural bottleneck lies in the feature space, not signal aggregation or decoding constraints.
    LP selection effectiveness is inversely associated with degeneracy rate across three datasets (58\% $\to$ fail, 21\% $\to$ $+0.42$~pp, 10\% $\to$ $+1.36$~pp at $p{<}0.001$); entity-type-level analysis provides stronger statistical grounding ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$, $n{=}8$).
\end{enumerate}

